WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:03.334
♪♪

00:00:03.334 --> 00:00:04.868
-I think you know
what time it is.

00:00:04.868 --> 00:00:06.133
[ Laughter ]

00:00:06.133 --> 00:00:14.167
Our writer, Ben Warheit,
wrote a piece on the McRib,

00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:19.601
a sketch in which he referred
to it as a seasonal menu item.

00:00:19.601 --> 00:00:21.300
Someone wrote,
"The McRib isn't seasonal.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.767
Seasonal would mean
that it comes out

00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:24.133
every year for the same season,

00:00:24.133 --> 00:00:26.067
but its availability
isn't determined by season,

00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:29.334
and McDonald's has gone
several years

00:00:29.334 --> 00:00:30.868
in-between releases."

00:00:30.868 --> 00:00:32.501
I knew that.

00:00:32.501 --> 00:00:35.400
I knew that when I read
Ben's first draft,

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.834
but I did not want
to interfere with his piece.

00:00:39.834 --> 00:00:41.634
I...

00:00:41.634 --> 00:00:45.334
I think this says
something about me as a boss,

00:00:45.334 --> 00:00:48.033
but I almost never

00:00:48.033 --> 00:00:53.501
give notes
on my writer's pieces.

00:00:53.501 --> 00:00:57.267
I don't like to interfere
with their vision.

00:00:57.267 --> 00:01:01.734
And I think the reason I don't
do that can be summed up

00:01:01.734 --> 00:01:05.534
in one word, which is apathy.

00:01:05.534 --> 00:01:08.968
[ Laughter ]

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:10.834
I said "inkblot tests."

00:01:10.834 --> 00:01:12.801
One of you told me inkblot tests

00:01:12.801 --> 00:01:15.634
are also known
as Rorschach tests.

00:01:15.634 --> 00:01:17.200
And this is interesting.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.901
Rorschach tests are also known
as inkblot tests.

00:01:21.901 --> 00:01:24.501
So I guess
we've reached an impasse.

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:28.000
[ Laughter ]

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.601
Talked about how we're gonna
write a song about the jackals,

00:01:30.601 --> 00:01:32.334
a "Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles"
type song,

00:01:32.334 --> 00:01:38.334
about, you know, grown-ass
online correction jackals.

00:01:38.334 --> 00:01:41.567
Zeroes in a half shell.

00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:42.734
Chuck Lorre --

00:01:42.734 --> 00:01:44.234
We talked about the fact,
Chuck Lorre,

00:01:44.234 --> 00:01:46.834
who created "Big Bang Theory,"
wrote the

00:01:46.834 --> 00:01:49.234
"Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtle"
theme song,

00:01:49.234 --> 00:01:53.934
but the "Big Bang Theory" song
was written by

00:01:53.934 --> 00:01:56.133
Barenaked Ladies.

00:01:56.133 --> 00:01:57.934
Yeah. Written and performed
by Barenaked Ladies.

00:01:57.934 --> 00:02:00.667
Chuck Lorre was paid $2,000

00:02:00.667 --> 00:02:03.534
to record
"Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles."

00:02:03.534 --> 00:02:07.434
Barenaked Ladies
were paid a million dollars.

00:02:07.434 --> 00:02:08.934
And I guess as soon
as they were paid

00:02:08.934 --> 00:02:11.701
the million dollars,
they spent it immediately.

00:02:11.701 --> 00:02:15.300
It was almost like they had
a list of things they would do

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:16.801
if they had
that much money.

00:02:16.801 --> 00:02:20.033
[ Laughter ]

00:02:20.033 --> 00:02:22.734
They bought John Merrick's
remains, Baze.

00:02:22.734 --> 00:02:27.100
[ Laughter ]

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.501
I think I finally
figured it out.

00:02:29.501 --> 00:02:35.334
If he's an Italian artist,
it's "Meekelangelo."

00:02:35.334 --> 00:02:39.501
If he's a turtle,
he's "Michael-angelo."

00:02:39.501 --> 00:02:42.501
If he's a magician,
he's Mike Angel.

00:02:42.501 --> 00:02:45.767
[ Laughter ]

00:02:45.767 --> 00:02:48.767
This is just one of my
celebrity stories

00:02:48.767 --> 00:02:50.767
within the body
of "Corrections."

00:02:50.767 --> 00:02:53.901
I met Criss Angel, Mindfreak.

00:02:53.901 --> 00:02:59.067
I met him at a rooftop
pool party in Las Vegas,

00:02:59.067 --> 00:03:00.901
i.e., his natural habitat.

00:03:00.901 --> 00:03:03.667
[ Laughter ]
And I, at the party,

00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:05.501
I was just going around
to groups of people

00:03:05.501 --> 00:03:07.767
and I was just doing
stand-up at them.

00:03:07.767 --> 00:03:09.868
I was just performing
stand-up at people

00:03:09.868 --> 00:03:11.100
who were just trying
to be at a party.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.601
No, wait.
I got that wrong.

00:03:12.601 --> 00:03:14.133
He was doing magic.

00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:17.100
[ Laughter ]

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.100
And Criss Angel, Mindfreak,

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:23.334
came up to me with a deck
of cards, and I took a card --

00:03:23.334 --> 00:03:25.834
you know, 9 of diamonds --
put it back in,

00:03:25.834 --> 00:03:29.501
and then he's doing
the start of the trick.

00:03:29.501 --> 00:03:33.133
And then, this group
of beautiful women

00:03:33.133 --> 00:03:36.467
enters the party,
and Criss Angel, Mindfreak,

00:03:36.467 --> 00:03:40.400
sees them
and just walks away from me.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.501
Mid-trick.

00:03:42.501 --> 00:03:43.667
Did not finish the trick.

00:03:43.667 --> 00:03:45.267
And then, later,
I walked over to him

00:03:45.267 --> 00:03:47.334
while he was doing --
doing it to someone else,

00:03:47.334 --> 00:03:49.701
and I said, "Hey, you know,
Criss Angel, Mindfreak,

00:03:49.701 --> 00:03:53.300
are you gonna ever finish?"
and he did this to me.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.067
This is what a magician
did to me when I asked him

00:03:55.067 --> 00:04:00.534
just to show me my card.
He went, "Be patient."

00:04:00.534 --> 00:04:02.534
And that's the last time
I ever saw...

00:04:02.534 --> 00:04:04.200
That's the Mindfreak.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.834
There's no end to the trick.

00:04:07.834 --> 00:04:09.334
Someone said,
which is some real --

00:04:09.334 --> 00:04:11.167
I think some parts of the world,

00:04:11.167 --> 00:04:12.567
they pronounce
things differently,

00:04:12.567 --> 00:04:15.400
that sloth is pronounced
"slowth."

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:18.367
I mean, come on, right?

00:04:18.367 --> 00:04:20.033
I'm not gonna start
saying "slowth."

00:04:20.033 --> 00:04:22.701
I would never remember it.

00:04:22.701 --> 00:04:26.667
That sloth rhymes with "slow."

00:04:26.667 --> 00:04:29.501
I mean, if there was
a mnemonic device of some kind.

00:04:29.501 --> 00:04:31.634
[ Laughter ]

00:04:31.634 --> 00:04:35.467
I talked about watching...

00:04:35.467 --> 00:04:39.367
our "slow-th" --
sloth, Serenity, drive away

00:04:39.367 --> 00:04:41.234
the last time she was here
and that she was driving,

00:04:41.234 --> 00:04:43.601
and I mimed
the steering wheel like this,

00:04:43.601 --> 00:04:47.334
and you all pointed out that
it would have been like this.

00:04:47.334 --> 00:04:50.367
Because she's
a two-fingered sloth.

00:04:50.367 --> 00:04:53.434
But it definitely was this.

00:04:53.434 --> 00:04:56.000
And at this point, I'm starting
to think this whole time

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.868
it was a guy in a sloth suit,
in which case

00:04:59.868 --> 00:05:01.934
I'm totally fine
that we had him here.

00:05:05.968 --> 00:05:10.534
I said sloths were becoming
a leitmotif for "Corrections,"

00:05:10.534 --> 00:05:14.467
and I was told that leitmotif
is only true for music.

00:05:14.467 --> 00:05:17.868
But to me, this is music.

00:05:17.868 --> 00:05:21.567
[ Laughter ]

00:05:21.567 --> 00:05:23.100
Every now and then,
some people say some things

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:27.067
that are not corrections
but rather just cruel comments.

00:05:27.067 --> 00:05:31.934
I wore this hat last week.

00:05:31.934 --> 00:05:37.100
I put this on,
and someone said

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:42.667
that I looked like "a grown,
old, worn-out Ash Ketchum."

00:05:42.667 --> 00:05:44.701
[ Laughter ]

00:05:44.701 --> 00:05:48.100
Okay. So, you, like me,
didn't know who that was.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:49.367
There you go.

00:05:49.367 --> 00:05:51.367
[ Laughter ]

00:05:51.367 --> 00:05:54.634
I'm just gonna go come around
and say, that's a fair hit.

00:05:54.634 --> 00:05:57.267
[ Laughter ]

00:05:57.267 --> 00:05:58.634
Someone else said --

00:05:58.634 --> 00:05:59.934
talking about
the length of "Corrections,"

00:05:59.934 --> 00:06:01.734
maybe it's getting
a little long.

00:06:01.734 --> 00:06:03.300
Someone said, "I put this on,
fell asleep,

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.200
and dude was still going."

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:06.601
[ Laughter ]

00:06:06.601 --> 00:06:09.601
Well, to you, I'd just like
to say, "Good morning."

00:06:09.601 --> 00:06:11.667
[ Laughter ]

00:06:11.667 --> 00:06:13.567
I was talking about
how people --

00:06:13.567 --> 00:06:15.434
I don't understand
why people choose to watch this

00:06:15.434 --> 00:06:16.767
if they don't like it,

00:06:16.767 --> 00:06:21.367
because there's such a wealth
of options on YouTube.

00:06:21.367 --> 00:06:24.033
And I -- I basically --

00:06:24.033 --> 00:06:26.267
I said something along the lines
of, "Are you the kind of person,

00:06:26.267 --> 00:06:27.501
when you walk
into a grocery store,

00:06:27.501 --> 00:06:29.501
you just buy
the first thing you see?

00:06:29.501 --> 00:06:31.167
You just go and take
the tuna fish?"

00:06:31.167 --> 00:06:34.133
and, boy, oh, boy,
you all jumped on that and said,

00:06:34.133 --> 00:06:36.734
"The fact --
This proves --

00:06:36.734 --> 00:06:42.434
This proves that you
are a disconnected elite

00:06:42.434 --> 00:06:44.267
that you think you can walk
into a grocery store

00:06:44.267 --> 00:06:46.367
and the first thing you see
is tuna fish.

00:06:46.367 --> 00:06:47.934
Someone who shops
for their own groceries

00:06:47.934 --> 00:06:49.734
would know, when you walk in,
it's produce.

00:06:49.734 --> 00:06:51.200
That's the first thing you see.

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:52.767
Well, I would just like
to tell you that I shop

00:06:52.767 --> 00:06:57.067
for my groceries.
Like everybody else.

00:06:57.067 --> 00:07:01.367
I just use the VIP entrance

00:07:01.367 --> 00:07:03.667
that opens right into seafood.

00:07:03.667 --> 00:07:09.100
[ Laughter ]

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:10.734
I said a dog house --
I corrected myself --

00:07:10.734 --> 00:07:12.567
a dog house
couldn't be hit by a meteorite

00:07:12.567 --> 00:07:15.601
because it's only a meteorite
once it hits the ground.

00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:17.334
It would've been hit
by a meteor.

00:07:17.334 --> 00:07:20.934
One of you very helpfully
pointed out, if it bounced,

00:07:20.934 --> 00:07:22.834
it's a meteorite.

00:07:22.834 --> 00:07:26.133
Meteor, meteorite,
dog house -- boom.

00:07:26.133 --> 00:07:28.868
Take it back.

00:07:28.868 --> 00:07:31.400
I said "Lego blocks" again
instead of "Lego bricks."

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:34.200
This is the mistake
I've maybe made the most often.

00:07:36.334 --> 00:07:37.467
At this point,
I'm starting to think

00:07:37.467 --> 00:07:40.200
the reason is,
I don't give a [bleep]

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:43.834
[ Laughter ]

00:07:43.834 --> 00:07:46.234
I said about Wally,
"We're not trying to create

00:07:46.234 --> 00:07:49.534
another Frankenstein."

00:07:49.534 --> 00:07:54.100
And -- biggest correction
of the week --

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:57.200
how many of you said,
"It's Frankenstein's monster."

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:58.467
Well, I was actually
doing something --

00:07:58.467 --> 00:08:02.434
I was giving
all of you, collectively,

00:08:02.434 --> 00:08:06.300
What's known as the
Jackal Briggs personality test.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:08.033
[ Laughter ]

00:08:08.033 --> 00:08:09.968
Where the person says
"Frankenstein"

00:08:09.968 --> 00:08:12.567
and waits to see
how many people say,

00:08:12.567 --> 00:08:15.801
"Uh, actually,
it's Frankenstein's monster."

00:08:15.801 --> 00:08:20.434
And when they do that, you know
they have a bad personality.

00:08:20.434 --> 00:08:22.868
[ Laughter ]

00:08:22.868 --> 00:08:24.200
I was talking about Shoemaker.

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:25.701
Shoemaker, not here.
Still out.

00:08:25.701 --> 00:08:27.968
Hoping to have him back
for next week's "Corrections."

00:08:27.968 --> 00:08:29.534
Fingers crossed.

00:08:29.534 --> 00:08:31.834
Shoemaker is recovering
from a surgery.

00:08:31.834 --> 00:08:33.534
We're a little worried
because he told us

00:08:33.534 --> 00:08:38.033
he was recovering
from the Ritz-Carlton

00:08:38.033 --> 00:08:39.067
in Puerto Rico.

00:08:39.067 --> 00:08:43.334
And, also,
our accountant called

00:08:43.334 --> 00:08:46.968
and said, "There's something --
There's some irregularities

00:08:46.968 --> 00:08:50.133
with the show's budget," so...

00:08:50.133 --> 00:08:53.100
He's been Zooming in.
He looks great.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:57.067
Like, tanner than
I've ever seen him, happy.

00:08:57.067 --> 00:08:58.367
Keeps saying --

00:08:58.367 --> 00:09:01.067
He's, like, drinking from --
He's got the umbrellas

00:09:01.067 --> 00:09:03.033
in his drinks,
and he says it's medicine.

00:09:03.033 --> 00:09:05.834
[ Laughter ]

00:09:05.834 --> 00:09:07.133
So I was talking
about Shoemaker,

00:09:07.133 --> 00:09:09.133
and some of you said,
"Why is he not at work?

00:09:09.133 --> 00:09:10.534
Because it doesn't seem
like he has a job

00:09:10.534 --> 00:09:11.968
where he needs two legs."

00:09:11.968 --> 00:09:13.767
And I said, "It's true --
you never see him

00:09:13.767 --> 00:09:15.734
running down the hallway,
like Holly Hunter,"

00:09:15.734 --> 00:09:17.567
which was a reference
to "Broadcast News."

00:09:17.567 --> 00:09:20.400
And then I caught myself --

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:22.234
in the moment --
and said, "Joan Cusack,"

00:09:22.234 --> 00:09:24.267
because I had mis-remembered it,
and then I caught it

00:09:24.267 --> 00:09:25.434
in the moment.

00:09:25.434 --> 00:09:26.968
It was pretty impressive.

00:09:26.968 --> 00:09:30.000
And let's just --
I just want to show you

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.400
a clip of that moment

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:33.834
so you can see how quickly
I caught it.

00:09:33.834 --> 00:09:35.534
I don't think any of us
can remember a time where,

00:09:35.534 --> 00:09:37.934
like, Shoemaker
was doing the Holly Hunter

00:09:37.934 --> 00:09:40.200
run down the hallway,
like, "The tapes!"

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:45.734
[ Laughter ]

00:09:45.734 --> 00:09:47.734
Oh [bleep]
It was Joan Cusack.

00:09:47.734 --> 00:09:49.934
-Ooh.
-Caught it.

00:09:49.934 --> 00:09:53.400
Pretty fast catch.

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:56.400
One of you,
in the comments section,

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:01.200
wrote, "It wasn't Holly Hunter.
It was Joan Cusack."

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:04.567
Then you followed it up with,
"Never mind. You caught it."

00:10:04.567 --> 00:10:09.501
[ Laughter ]

00:10:09.501 --> 00:10:11.033
What did you do?

00:10:11.033 --> 00:10:12.901
[ Laughter ]

00:10:12.901 --> 00:10:15.801
Did you stop it
after I made the mistake,

00:10:15.801 --> 00:10:18.934
or did,
in that seven seconds,

00:10:18.934 --> 00:10:21.834
did you jackal-paw out
your correction?

00:10:21.834 --> 00:10:24.934
Now let me just say...

00:10:24.934 --> 00:10:29.734
I tip my cap,
which I'm told is this...

00:10:29.734 --> 00:10:32.601
to the fact that you're
owning up to your mistake.

00:10:32.601 --> 00:10:37.234
But, my God, the speed
you people want to be right.

00:10:37.234 --> 00:10:39.234
[ Laughter ]

00:10:43.467 --> 00:10:45.801
All right.

00:10:45.801 --> 00:10:50.267
We talked about E.B. White's
classic book, "Stuart Little."

00:10:50.267 --> 00:10:52.701
I mentioned that one of the many
weird details in the movie

00:10:52.701 --> 00:10:55.100
is that Stuart,
the mouse, is born --

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:57.000
Sorry. One of the weird details
in the book --

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.133
this is an important
clarification -- in the book

00:10:59.133 --> 00:11:01.734
is that Stuart the mouse is born
to adult parents.

00:11:01.734 --> 00:11:04.734
Many of you jumped in saying
the Littles adopted Stuart,

00:11:04.734 --> 00:11:06.601
which is what they did
in the movie.

00:11:06.601 --> 00:11:08.968
And when you, as a jackal,

00:11:08.968 --> 00:11:10.934
correct someone
with a movie fact

00:11:10.934 --> 00:11:13.067
when they're, in fact,
talking about the book,

00:11:13.067 --> 00:11:16.467
you played yourself.

00:11:16.467 --> 00:11:19.334
Here's an amazing fact, though.
Do you know who wrote

00:11:19.334 --> 00:11:21.934
the screenplay
for "Stuart Little"?

00:11:21.934 --> 00:11:23.968
M. Night Shyamalan.

00:11:23.968 --> 00:11:25.501
That is true.
How about --

00:11:25.501 --> 00:11:27.100
How is that for a twist?

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:28.467
[ Laughter ]

00:11:28.467 --> 00:11:32.667
And he changed two --
two major changes from the book

00:11:32.667 --> 00:11:34.934
to the movie
in the M. Night screenplay.

00:11:34.934 --> 00:11:37.234
One is that Stuart was not born

00:11:37.234 --> 00:11:38.801
to the Littles.
He was adopted.

00:11:38.801 --> 00:11:40.534
And, two,
at the end of the movie --

00:11:40.534 --> 00:11:41.400
this is not in the book --

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:42.467
at the end of the movie,
the dad,

00:11:42.467 --> 00:11:44.267
Frederick Little,
says to his son,

00:11:44.267 --> 00:11:47.000
"Isn't it weird that everyone

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:50.334
is cool with the fact that
we have a mouse for a child?"

00:11:50.334 --> 00:11:54.300
and Stuart says, "That's because
you've been dead for 10 years."

00:11:54.300 --> 00:12:00.634
[ Laughter ]

00:12:00.634 --> 00:12:03.767
Some of you said that I,
by talking about

00:12:03.767 --> 00:12:05.334
the ending of the book,
I ruined the ending

00:12:05.334 --> 00:12:06.901
of "Stuart Little."
That's not possible

00:12:06.901 --> 00:12:09.934
because there is no ending
to "Stuart Little."

00:12:09.934 --> 00:12:11.934
Just peters off.

00:12:14.501 --> 00:12:18.667
I told the story about how
I wrote some extra lines

00:12:18.667 --> 00:12:22.834
in my head to my children
about how Stuart, at the end,

00:12:22.834 --> 00:12:26.200
found Margalo, the bird,
he was looking for in the book.

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:28.801
And now this is maybe the best
correction I've ever seen.

00:12:28.801 --> 00:12:31.501
You guys...

00:12:31.501 --> 00:12:34.767
Someone wrote to us on Facebook.

00:12:34.767 --> 00:12:38.200
And this is a true story --
they sent -- they sent stuff.

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:41.267
Hold on.

00:12:41.267 --> 00:12:43.167
This was Andrew Frischman.
Thank you so much

00:12:43.167 --> 00:12:45.901
for doing this.
I hope you're not catfishing us.

00:12:45.901 --> 00:12:48.667
I hope this is a real story.

00:12:48.667 --> 00:12:50.300
He wrote, "In 1953 --"

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:52.534
And, again, my whole issue

00:12:52.534 --> 00:12:54.167
with "Stuart Little,"
which was a book I loved,

00:12:54.167 --> 00:12:55.567
my kids loved, is,
at the end of the book,

00:12:55.567 --> 00:12:56.968
just sort of --
there's no ending.

00:12:56.968 --> 00:13:00.367
"In 1953, my mother,
Kay Berthold,

00:13:00.367 --> 00:13:02.067
was about to turn 10 years old

00:13:02.067 --> 00:13:04.000
when she read
'Stuart Little,' by E.B. White,

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:06.434
and like Seth's children,
she was upset by the ending.

00:13:06.434 --> 00:13:08.667
So she wrote a letter
to E.B. White,

00:13:08.667 --> 00:13:11.167
and E.B. White wrote her back.

00:13:11.167 --> 00:13:12.501
Her letter said,
'Dear Mr. White,

00:13:12.501 --> 00:13:14.567
I love "Stuart Little,"
but I don't like the ending

00:13:14.567 --> 00:13:16.968
because I don't know whether
he's going to get there or not.

00:13:16.968 --> 00:13:19.200
Kay Berthold.'
And E.B. White's --"

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:21.534
And that's --
by the way, he sent us this.

00:13:21.534 --> 00:13:22.534
There you go.

00:13:22.534 --> 00:13:25.200
That's what that looked like
in 1953.

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:30.901
"And E.B. White wrote back
March 23, 1953,

00:13:30.901 --> 00:13:32.868
and wrote, 'Dear Kay,

00:13:32.868 --> 00:13:34.400
Thanks for your letter
about "Stuart Little."

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:36.300
Sorry the end
doesn't sound right to you.

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:38.968
It's too late to change it now,
even if I wanted to,

00:13:38.968 --> 00:13:41.601
and I don't.
All I can tell you is that

00:13:41.601 --> 00:13:44.601
Stuart was driving
in a good car, loved to travel,

00:13:44.601 --> 00:13:46.234
and was headed
in the right direction.

00:13:46.234 --> 00:13:49.033
I think it's a fine thing to be
headed in the right direction

00:13:49.033 --> 00:13:51.667
whether you find
what you're looking for or not.

00:13:51.667 --> 00:13:55.467
Sincerely, E.B. White.'"

00:13:55.467 --> 00:13:57.834
Which is very beautiful.

00:13:57.834 --> 00:14:00.634
And I read that
and was deeply moved,

00:14:00.634 --> 00:14:03.868
and I
immediately knew the mistake

00:14:03.868 --> 00:14:10.667
I had made in being critical
of Mr. White's amazing work.

00:14:10.667 --> 00:14:14.234
But then I read on,
and Andrew added,

00:14:14.234 --> 00:14:16.667
"So my correction
is that even as Seth was trying

00:14:16.667 --> 00:14:18.501
to make the book
more palatable to his children,

00:14:18.501 --> 00:14:20.534
Seth, in fact,
ruined the author's very clear,

00:14:20.534 --> 00:14:23.067
explicit intention
to teach a broader life lesson.

00:14:23.067 --> 00:14:25.901
While I'm sure that Seth's book
is destined to become a classic,

00:14:25.901 --> 00:14:27.267
it seems that
Seth would be best off

00:14:27.267 --> 00:14:29.033
leaving the endings of books
like 'Stuart Little'

00:14:29.033 --> 00:14:30.167
to the original authors."

00:14:30.167 --> 00:14:31.300
Okay.

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:33.901
[ Laughter ]

00:14:33.901 --> 00:14:35.267
Little much.

00:14:35.267 --> 00:14:39.868
[ Laughter ]

00:14:39.868 --> 00:14:42.667
I do, though --
I have a book coming out,

00:14:42.667 --> 00:14:44.033
"I'm Not Scared, You're Scared."

00:14:44.033 --> 00:14:45.267
And this is very exciting.

00:14:45.267 --> 00:14:47.701
I talked to the publishers.

00:14:47.701 --> 00:14:49.601
And we are going to release
two different versions of it.

00:14:49.601 --> 00:14:52.367
There's the original version,
which is the version I wrote.

00:14:52.367 --> 00:14:54.667
And then there's going to be
an E.B. White version,

00:14:54.667 --> 00:14:56.067
which is the same version,

00:14:56.067 --> 00:14:57.667
but we're going to rip
the last three pages out of it.

00:14:57.667 --> 00:15:05.601
[ Laughter ]

00:15:05.601 --> 00:15:08.067
I mean, this is
an amazing letter.

00:15:08.067 --> 00:15:09.334
It's so amazing.
Thank you so much

00:15:09.334 --> 00:15:10.734
for sending it.
And thank you, also,

00:15:10.734 --> 00:15:12.067
for sending --

00:15:12.067 --> 00:15:13.734
This was cool -- because
this is obviously a photocopy.

00:15:13.734 --> 00:15:15.801
But he sent us...

00:15:15.801 --> 00:15:19.367
There was another...
You guys see this?

00:15:19.367 --> 00:15:21.300
Which said, "Dear Kay,

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:22.934
I wanted to follow up
with one more thought.

00:15:22.934 --> 00:15:25.234
People who say, 'Actually,
it's Frankenstein's monster,'

00:15:25.234 --> 00:15:26.701
are huge ass [bleep]."

00:15:26.701 --> 00:15:30.434
[ Laughter ]

00:15:30.434 --> 00:15:32.834
"Sincerely, E.B. White.

00:15:32.834 --> 00:15:35.000
P.S., Isn't it [bleep] up

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:37.000
that two humans
had a kid mouse?"

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:39.834
[ Laughter ]

00:15:39.834 --> 00:15:41.334
You can tell it's -- Look.

00:15:41.334 --> 00:15:44.834
You can tell, it's --
yeah, it's the day after.

00:15:44.834 --> 00:15:48.000
And that's obviously
an old letter.

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:49.868
Look at these --
this discoloration.

00:15:49.868 --> 00:15:52.801
[ Laughter ]

00:15:52.801 --> 00:15:55.868
That's something
that only happens in time.

00:15:55.868 --> 00:15:58.167
[ Laughter ]

00:15:58.167 --> 00:16:00.167
I feel -- felt like we went
a little quicker today.

00:16:00.167 --> 00:16:01.767
I think that's good.
I think we're all just, like,

00:16:01.767 --> 00:16:04.100
very excited for Shoemaker
to come back.

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:06.033
Um, uh...

00:16:06.033 --> 00:16:10.901
Went over everything we had.

00:16:10.901 --> 00:16:15.734
And...

00:16:15.734 --> 00:16:17.467
Oh [bleep] Criss Angel.

